1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:08,800
Welcome to the Subclub podcast, a show dedicated to the best practices for building and growing app businesses.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:19,360
We sit down with the entrepreneurs, investors and builders behind the most successful apps in the world to learn from their successes and failures.

3
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:22,560
Subclub is brought to you by RevenueCat.

4
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:32,880
Thousands of the world's best apps trust RevenueCat to power in app purchases, manage customers and grow revenue across iOS, Android and the web.

5
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,240
You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

6
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:51,040
Hello, I'm your host, David Bernard, and my guest today is Alex Prasad, CEO of V one Sports, the leader in video golf swing analysis software and seamless video lessons.

7
00:00:51,520 --> 00:01:04,240
On the podcast, I talk with Alex about the power of user segmentation, executing bold strategic shifts, and why imaginary customer conversations are sometimes better than real ones.

8
00:01:04,960 --> 00:01:07,360
Hey Alex, thank you so much for joining me on the podcast today.

9
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:08,640
Really looking forward to chatting.

10
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:09,680
Thanks for having me, David.

11
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:14,720
Super excited to share our story and hopefully there's a a learning or two that's applicable to your listeners.

12
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:15,600
I think there will be.

13
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:17,520
That's why I wanted to have you on the podcast.

14
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:24,240
The interesting thing about V one Sports and what I wanted to focus on was how, and I think so many apps are going to relate to this.

15
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:31,680
It's like V one Sports had amazing early product market fit and built a great business around that and then went through the wilderness.

16
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:41,440
So let's kick it off with what that early product market fit was and then we'll go through how you came in and the things you did to really get it back into a growth state.

17
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:42,080
Yeah.

18
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:48,960
So this company is over twenty years old, maybe closer to thirty, which is crazy to think about from a technology company perspective, not much less than thirty.

19
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:49,840
Not a lot lasts that long, basically.

20
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:50,160
Right.

21
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:58,480
And the first innovation was being able to send video over the internet before really anyone else could and applying it to this golf context.

22
00:01:58,720 --> 00:02:05,200
So I know today everyone streaming 4K for NFL games and their quad screens on YouTube TV like hiking through the woods, right?

23
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:07,920
But that was not the case twenty plus years ago, right?

24
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:16,080
And then the second was telestrating on that video so that instructors could do a video analysis, whether in person or purely remotely and send it right over the internet.

25
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:21,200
So that innovation won roughly 30% market share in North America among golf instructors.

26
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:26,240
That's our B2B side of the business, RevenueCats working on the consumer side side, we'll be talking mostly about that.

27
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:28,960
But that became less innovative over time, like every innovation, right?

28
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:32,720
And so it won that market share, and then there was a plateau for a while in terms of growth.

29
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:40,800
So, especially on the consumer side, learned a lot of things to then start to kickstart whatever, maybe phase two, whatever you want to call it, of growth.

30
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:45,600
And not in a straight line, not because we're so smart, but through a lot of trial and error and a lot of learnings.

31
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:46,720
Let's talk through that.

32
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:50,320
So, what was the state of things when you joined the company?

33
00:02:50,480 --> 00:03:00,480
And then let's start talking through that early, what it took to take this great business, but great business that had kind of plateaued, and get it back to growth.

34
00:03:00,960 --> 00:03:01,040
Yeah.

35
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:07,040
Well, if you think about a thirty year arc in technology, I like to step through phases.

36
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:10,640
So late nineties, like who's the best company?

37
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:13,040
Ford, and then maybe Microsoft, right?

38
00:03:13,280 --> 00:03:16,240
So you go from, let's sell a lot of stuff, Ford, right?

39
00:03:16,480 --> 00:03:21,440
Like big, expensive machines to Microsoft, let's license software, right?

40
00:03:21,680 --> 00:03:22,240
And deploy that.

41
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:25,760
So here's your CD and install it on x number of computers, whatever, right?

42
00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:29,440
And then you start to move, I'm skipping like lots of shades of gray here, right?

43
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:31,200
You know, into like a subscription model.

44
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:36,640
But so when there's a business that's of that age, you have remnants of each of those models.

45
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:39,440
And I would say now we're like in a platform model, right?

46
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:44,320
With two sides of a marketplace and so on and so forth is the best, you know, the best in quotes, right?

47
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:45,200
The most interesting thing.

48
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:48,960
And maybe we've actually aged out of that and now it's AI, something, right?

49
00:03:49,680 --> 00:03:59,280
But so, yeah, it was a puzzle because you had vestiges of each of these business models, which were the best practices at the time lying about, basically.

50
00:03:59,520 --> 00:04:04,000
And so we needed to put a stake in the ground and say, which of these are we really?

51
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:07,360
Which is antiquated by age or maybe not still pertinent?

52
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:14,000
If you think about these users, I mean, we have high 80 percent retention on the B2B side of the business.

53
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:16,240
So these guys are happy where they are.

54
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:17,200
They like the thing.

55
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:19,920
Then they don't really want fundamental, right?

56
00:04:20,080 --> 00:04:24,240
That behavior, regardless of what we're doing to them, if you will, means like, I don't really want to move, right?

57
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:25,200
I'm happy where I am.

58
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:25,520
Thank you.

59
00:04:25,760 --> 00:04:27,760
Just keep delivering this thing to me without disruption.

60
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:33,680
So we had to look at those various business models and choose one and then try to harmonize around it.

61
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:47,520
And so especially on the consumer side, because the business started purely B2B, I would say the strategy was, I don't want to say immature, that sounds negative, not the point, but it hadn't been focused on as to how these pieces of the puzzle go together, right?

62
00:04:47,680 --> 00:04:48,640
So not surprising.

63
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:54,560
that opportunistic line of business plateaued because it wasn't really the focus as a starting point.

64
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:56,320
It wasn't the genesis of the company.

65
00:04:56,560 --> 00:05:08,400
It sounds like this is why you joined, as you saw that there was like a ton of potential, but what did the business look like at that point, like size and users, and you already mentioned eighty percent retention, which is insane.

66
00:05:08,960 --> 00:05:09,040
Yeah.

67
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:18,080
So thirty percent market share on the B2B business has not changed a ton over the last I've been with the company a little bit over four years now, the annual contract values increasing.

68
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:19,440
That's the exciting story there.

69
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:22,560
Again, not really our focus on the consumer side.

70
00:05:22,720 --> 00:05:34,640
Again, it's not just like the order of events was that the consumer thing was opportunistic, but it had never, in my opinion, been thought about rethought once it reached a certain size.

71
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:42,160
So because of that B2B dynamic, the focus was really not disrupting the instructor consumer relationship.

72
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:48,640
So some instructors would have problems with the fact that we could sell directly to their customers that were on the platform.

73
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:50,240
So we rethought that.

74
00:05:50,400 --> 00:06:07,840
We made some bold decisions which did create some friction on that B2B side, but we basically switched from a freemium model, in my opinion, in a direct to consumer business is probably the most complicated monetization strategy you can have.

75
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:13,920
And we've got to be really clear thesis statement, really clear metrics, really know where to draw these lines, right?

76
00:06:14,240 --> 00:06:18,960
Otherwise, no pun intended for a video teletransmission company, draw the lines, father, dad joke, right?

77
00:06:19,280 --> 00:06:27,600
So, but it hadn't that freemium strategy had been really, I think, deployed more defensively to like keep the pros happy, if you will.

78
00:06:28,160 --> 00:06:32,240
And still today, the pros, students, athletes can join the platform.

79
00:06:32,720 --> 00:06:35,680
We're never going to disrupt with a paywall, the interaction between the two.

80
00:06:36,400 --> 00:06:38,640
It's like a touchstone third rail.

81
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:40,560
You're never going to change that.

82
00:06:40,640 --> 00:06:42,960
It's a fundamental tenant of our business, if you will.

83
00:06:43,600 --> 00:06:52,480
But we did rethink that freemium business model because the thesis statement, I think, was fairly weak to be blunt because it wasn't, again, it was like an add on line of business, right?

84
00:06:52,640 --> 00:06:53,520
It wasn't the main event.

85
00:06:53,840 --> 00:06:55,680
So it didn't get that level of thinking.

86
00:06:56,080 --> 00:06:59,200
When something's opportunistic, you attack it and you take the money that's there, right?

87
00:06:59,680 --> 00:07:03,280
But that's very different than being methodical and thinking about it from scratch.

88
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:12,720
I want to interject because I think a lot of apps can relate and maybe I need to help point this out, but so many apps, if they're four, five, six years old.

89
00:07:12,720 --> 00:07:13,280
4, 5, 6 years old.

90
00:07:13,440 --> 00:07:21,840
They don't have as much kind of business strategy debt as V one Sports did, but they probably have more than they realize.

91
00:07:22,160 --> 00:07:29,600
There are features that they give away for free in their freemium strategy that maybe are higher value that they should be putting behind a paywall.

92
00:07:30,160 --> 00:07:34,560
There are ways they do things that haven't been rethought in years.

93
00:07:35,200 --> 00:07:37,920
And to your point earlier, things have changed so much.

94
00:07:38,400 --> 00:07:41,040
Consumer willingness to pay via subscription has changed.

95
00:07:41,600 --> 00:07:43,120
Onboarding patterns have changed.

96
00:07:43,440 --> 00:07:47,840
Even like willingness to pay higher prices are surprisingly doing better and better.

97
00:07:48,320 --> 00:07:56,560
If you can deliver the value, consumers are more willing to pay higher prices for software these days than I ever thought consumers would be willing to pay for.

98
00:07:57,200 --> 00:08:02,880
So I think this is how if you're listening in and you're like, what does this like all that have to do with my business?

99
00:08:03,440 --> 00:08:20,320
I think this is the meat of it is that if you've been around and your business has plateaued at all, or even if you've just been around a bit and are still growing but want to grow faster, like taking a minute to reevaluate some of these like core aspects of the business can produce incredible fruit.

100
00:08:20,480 --> 00:08:21,520
So then what was your process?

101
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:23,600
Like how did you gather data, talk to users?

102
00:08:23,920 --> 00:08:24,560
What did it look like?

103
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:30,000
It looked like to figure out where those opportunities were to shift the business strategy.

104
00:08:30,480 --> 00:08:31,600
Well, how much time do we have?

105
00:08:33,040 --> 00:08:46,160
So, I'm a corporate lawyer by training and that, you know, perverted or rewired or whatever adjectives you want to use, my brain, not a litigator, no one makes TV shows about corporate attorneys, but the first was just to challenge our assumptions.

106
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:52,000
So one of our, it's not a core value, but it's just lurking outside of our core values is nobody knows anything.

107
00:08:52,480 --> 00:08:53,360
I know nobody, right?

108
00:08:53,600 --> 00:08:54,640
I believe in experience shares.

109
00:08:54,880 --> 00:08:56,960
I'm not giving advice, telling you our experience.

110
00:08:57,200 --> 00:08:58,960
Here's what worked for us, here's what didn't.

111
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:00,240
Apply it to your situation, right?

112
00:09:00,320 --> 00:09:06,640
I don't know enough about your situation to tell you, right, that my approach is right or wrong, our approach is right or wrong.

113
00:09:06,960 --> 00:09:10,080
And then another kind of corporate lawyer thing, it's a logical syllogism.

114
00:09:10,320 --> 00:09:16,320
Everything that we're doing, what are these implicit assumptions that we're making to make decisions, right?

115
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:21,040
And the more time that passes, the more dated those like bedrock assumptions are.

116
00:09:21,600 --> 00:09:36,000
I think one of the things that our team is most proud of is it feels like we've been in the last four years, like fifteen different companies, completely disorients our board, which is a whole different masterclass that someone needs to give me, but I view that as a sign of progress and success.

117
00:09:36,560 --> 00:09:42,880
So there's lots of times we actually come back to something that we may have done previously and someone says, well, we already tried that.

118
00:09:42,960 --> 00:09:44,960
And I go, whoa, but that was eighteen months ago.

119
00:09:45,440 --> 00:09:47,680
And think about all the things we've learned, let's go back to.

120
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:50,720
So I'm a big fan of why are you where you are?

121
00:09:51,200 --> 00:09:55,200
Can you go back and critically evaluate that and are those still true?

122
00:09:55,600 --> 00:09:57,840
Sometimes they might just be truisms, right?

123
00:09:58,160 --> 00:09:59,840
And you might just say, check, check, check.

124
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:05,040
For instance, for us, we're never going to make the student pay to interact with their instructor.

125
00:10:05,200 --> 00:10:06,080
We're just not going to do it.

126
00:10:06,480 --> 00:10:08,320
We go back every once in a while and say, well, we could.

127
00:10:08,720 --> 00:10:10,800
And we go, no, but that violates our core value.

128
00:10:10,880 --> 00:10:12,240
You know, and these people have a long relationship with us.

129
00:10:12,320 --> 00:10:13,680
We're not on the pro side, right?

130
00:10:13,840 --> 00:10:14,800
We're not going to disrupt that.

131
00:10:14,880 --> 00:10:15,200
Okay, great.

132
00:10:15,440 --> 00:10:16,320
That one hasn't moved.

133
00:10:16,560 --> 00:10:17,440
But what about these other things?

134
00:10:18,080 --> 00:10:20,960
So first for us was just go back to the beginning.

135
00:10:21,760 --> 00:10:25,520
And so free trial is just simpler, right?

136
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:29,760
We couldn't reconstruct the thesis statement behind the freemium strategy.

137
00:10:29,920 --> 00:10:33,440
So we said we need to make things simpler and like get back to the basics.

138
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:38,640
And by the way, if you switch from free trial or from freemium to free trial, you're just going to make more money doing it.

139
00:10:38,960 --> 00:10:40,400
You might burn users, a different story.

140
00:10:40,560 --> 00:10:41,040
We can talk about that.

141
00:10:41,120 --> 00:10:42,080
And bad reviews, whatever.

142
00:10:42,160 --> 00:10:43,120
So there are costs, right?

143
00:10:43,440 --> 00:10:47,280
So that was, I don't say I use the word easy a lot when I think I mean simple.

144
00:10:47,680 --> 00:10:48,000
That was simple.

145
00:10:48,240 --> 00:10:51,200
It was A or B, you have to choose a door, pick one, right?

146
00:10:51,920 --> 00:10:58,880
But we decided basically to retreat to move forward, meaning there's a lot of assumptions that are stacked here, and we don't really know how many of these are true.

147
00:10:59,200 --> 00:11:08,880
Where is a simple place to start explaining the universe that we're living in, and then proceed from there and try to meticulously brick by brick, right, lay these down so you have a solid foundation.

148
00:11:09,280 --> 00:11:22,880
And again, because we've had such high turnover, a feature and a bug in some ways, and so institutional knowledge got eroded to some extent, it was easier to do that because there weren't many sacred cows to us, but also harder because there was fear around, well, what are we disrupting, right?

149
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:24,720
Because we didn't have that full knowledge.

150
00:11:24,880 --> 00:11:32,480
So that was a start at least simplifying things so we could list the four or five assumptions that we thought we were operating on and then building on those.

151
00:11:32,960 --> 00:11:33,040
Yeah.

152
00:11:33,600 --> 00:11:35,120
Let's talk about that one assumption then.

153
00:11:35,280 --> 00:11:46,160
So it sounds like people would open the app, go through onboarding and then be able to use the app and certain features for free, but was there a premium tier at that point and what did that look like?

154
00:11:46,400 --> 00:11:51,440
And then when you say switch to free trial, is there no longer any free usage of the app?

155
00:11:51,760 --> 00:11:53,040
Like you have to pay to use the app?

156
00:11:53,200 --> 00:11:55,200
Like what data, what hypothesis did you have?

157
00:11:55,360 --> 00:11:56,000
How did you test it?

158
00:11:56,480 --> 00:11:57,280
This is a transition.

159
00:11:57,360 --> 00:12:00,960
Maybe a lot of I did this in my weather app, so I've already talked about this on the podcast.

160
00:12:02,080 --> 00:12:05,440
It was always intended to be short term, but it worked really well in the short term.

161
00:12:05,920 --> 00:12:11,600
And so now I'm going to revisit freemium once we get the product to a certain point where the costs align and things like that.

162
00:12:11,840 --> 00:12:12,720
And we're in a different market.

163
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:17,120
So like you said, experience is something you need to learn from but not always directly apply.

164
00:12:17,360 --> 00:12:19,280
But anyway, so yeah, what did you mean by freemium?

165
00:12:19,440 --> 00:12:20,320
What did it look like in the past?

166
00:12:20,480 --> 00:12:25,040
And then what were the kind of user research and data that kind of helped inform the hypothesis?

167
00:12:25,360 --> 00:12:26,720
And then how did you validate that?

168
00:12:27,280 --> 00:12:27,360
Yeah.

169
00:12:27,840 --> 00:12:33,360
So there's a limited number of ways to improve your golf swing, just like fundamentally, right?

170
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,640
So you could work with someone that's working with a coach, right?

171
00:12:37,440 --> 00:12:39,120
You can look at your own swing with video.

172
00:12:39,440 --> 00:12:44,560
We view that as like foundational elemental if you want to improve any athletic thing.

173
00:12:44,720 --> 00:12:52,640
If you're not going to watch yourself doing it, your level of seriousness about improving is, in our view, because we're thinking about our ICP, right, reduced significantly.

174
00:12:52,800 --> 00:12:55,520
So you're not really the droids we're looking for, probably, right?

175
00:12:56,160 --> 00:13:03,840
And so video telestrating goes along with that and being able to look repeatedly, right, this swing, what's the same, what's different, and what's a flaw based on what I know or what we're teaching you.

176
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:05,040
And then looking at content.

177
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:06,480
So there's only three or four things.

178
00:13:07,120 --> 00:13:11,280
So we were allowing you to do basic telestrating in this freemium, but not advanced.

179
00:13:11,680 --> 00:13:14,720
We thought that was way too thinin of a hair to slice, right?

180
00:13:14,880 --> 00:13:15,280
To split.

181
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:15,840
There's no way.

182
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:17,280
You're either in or out, right?

183
00:13:17,840 --> 00:13:28,080
And so when we still have way more free users, like three X, four X, like monthly active users, and we do paid, basically we just split up the constituent parts, right?

184
00:13:28,240 --> 00:13:36,240
So the interaction with the pro, because the pros are generating about a third of our new registered users on a daily basis are coming from this relationship.

185
00:13:36,480 --> 00:13:38,240
So yeah, we don't want to disrupt this.

186
00:13:38,320 --> 00:13:39,200
So what does that mean today?

187
00:13:39,680 --> 00:13:42,720
Send videos to your coach, go back and forth with them, interact with them.

188
00:13:42,880 --> 00:13:45,680
So the pro's experience and your experience, hey, there's this free app.

189
00:13:45,920 --> 00:13:47,600
This is what you use to interact with me, right?

190
00:13:47,920 --> 00:13:48,240
Let's go.

191
00:13:48,480 --> 00:13:48,640
Great.

192
00:13:49,280 --> 00:13:54,160
But then there are three quarters of the people that are not coming through that channel, right?

193
00:13:54,400 --> 00:13:57,040
So we kind of tease segmentation, we can talk more about that.

194
00:13:57,360 --> 00:14:00,000
But the three quarters is where there's all this volume here, right?

195
00:14:00,240 --> 00:14:01,200
Three X of volume.

196
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:07,920
So that's some of the decision making progress or process, just at the starting point, who are you and how did you come here, right?

197
00:14:08,080 --> 00:14:09,440
You came through a pro or you didn't.

198
00:14:09,520 --> 00:14:10,160
I'll just start there.

199
00:14:10,320 --> 00:14:17,280
By the way, when you're trying to remodel the plane while it's flying, doing some basic things like that is hard, don't get me wrong.

200
00:14:17,360 --> 00:14:23,600
And we're at a place now where we actually think we do that well, but that was like the problem two years ago.

201
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:24,880
So that was a starting point.

202
00:14:25,440 --> 00:14:29,840
And then it was, is it reasonable to have to pay for that?

203
00:14:30,080 --> 00:14:33,840
Like, how much convincing are we doing with the freemium tools?

204
00:14:34,320 --> 00:14:36,800
And we can talk about data and like, when do you know to go or not?

205
00:14:37,040 --> 00:14:39,120
That's a good thing that we've talked about previously, David.

206
00:14:39,520 --> 00:14:53,040
But that was just instinct, I think, largely, like when we don't know and we don't have data to point it out, we try to retreat, make things simpler so we like, are on more solid footing.

207
00:14:53,520 --> 00:14:57,840
Obviously not like you burn a bunch of money or stop charging people or whatever.

208
00:14:57,920 --> 00:14:58,000
Right?

209
00:14:58,320 --> 00:15:01,120
But retreat, like, it's too a golf app.

210
00:15:01,280 --> 00:15:02,800
Like, this isn't that complicated, right?

211
00:15:03,040 --> 00:15:08,480
But if we don't understand what's going on, like adding in more and more factors is not going to make it easier, it's going to make it harder, right?

212
00:15:08,720 --> 00:15:09,680
So let's simplify this thing.

213
00:15:09,840 --> 00:15:10,720
Here's the thing, do you want it?

214
00:15:10,880 --> 00:15:11,840
If you do, you'll pay us.

215
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:13,840
If you don't, yeah, should you have a free trial?

216
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:15,600
Sure, try it out before you buy, right?

217
00:15:15,920 --> 00:15:24,800
But we'd really just try to simplify as a starting point, and that was more instinct, and really not instinct about what the best reaction would be in the market, instinct that we didn't know.

218
00:15:25,680 --> 00:15:30,480
So what are we supposed to do if we're driving this vehicle, we're not in control of it, what's the first thing you do?

219
00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:31,280
Slow it down.

220
00:15:31,920 --> 00:15:33,040
Try to understand what's going on.

221
00:15:33,040 --> 00:15:35,920
Because if we just keep going faster, we're going to hit something eventually, right?

222
00:15:36,480 --> 00:15:37,760
So that's the way we thought about that.

223
00:15:37,920 --> 00:15:42,080
And then as we got more fine tuned, certainly data and instinct or whatever started to come into play.

224
00:15:42,240 --> 00:15:45,440
But at the start it was simplify and respect the existing relationships.

225
00:15:45,760 --> 00:15:46,960
I think we did that at least.

226
00:15:47,440 --> 00:15:48,880
What did the gathering data look like?

227
00:15:49,120 --> 00:15:57,520
What did it look like talking to users, having conversations, figuring out how you could make this transition from freemium to the free trial?

228
00:15:58,160 --> 00:16:06,000
That transition versus I think it's about maturity of a company, like in where you are, that was more of a naked bet to be blunt, right?

229
00:16:06,400 --> 00:16:09,360
We just knew what we didn't know, and so we had to make a leap.

230
00:16:09,920 --> 00:16:11,040
But it taught us a lot.

231
00:16:11,600 --> 00:16:12,640
So we got all these reviews.

232
00:16:13,120 --> 00:16:17,200
of people who were in the freemium and all of a sudden now some of their things, right, had to be paid.

233
00:16:17,600 --> 00:16:18,400
And you get these reviews.

234
00:16:18,480 --> 00:16:19,840
It's in lots of one star reviews.

235
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:26,400
If you go back in the V one Golf app in the iOS store and you go back two years, you know, it was a massacre, right?

236
00:16:27,200 --> 00:16:32,720
And the responses were, I've gotten this for free for nine years and now I have to pay for it.

237
00:16:33,120 --> 00:16:33,840
I hate these guys.

238
00:16:34,160 --> 00:16:37,920
I like to say, the more time I spend in other people's shoes, the more effective I have been for the day.

239
00:16:38,080 --> 00:16:40,960
Because it really, at the end of the day, it doesn't matter what we want.

240
00:16:41,200 --> 00:16:43,200
It matters what our customers want, right?

241
00:16:43,440 --> 00:16:45,680
Because we are trying to get our customers to open our wallet.

242
00:16:45,920 --> 00:16:50,480
And so I like to, and a lot of calls that we have have are thinking things through and I'm not saying anything.

243
00:16:50,880 --> 00:16:51,360
What are you doing?

244
00:16:51,440 --> 00:16:54,320
I'm like, I'm playing out, I'm having a fake conversation with a real person.

245
00:16:54,640 --> 00:17:00,000
So I'm having a conversation with this guy who's leaving me a one star review saying, I got this free for nine years and now I have to pay.

246
00:17:00,560 --> 00:17:02,560
I don't think I had that conversation in real life.

247
00:17:02,800 --> 00:17:03,760
I think the guy pays me.

248
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:14,000
You don't come to the bar and get a free drink and then come again next week because the barman likes you or whatever and go, well, yesterday or last week you gave me a free drink, so I'm entitled to another.

249
00:17:14,560 --> 00:17:15,360
Hey, thanks for the favor.

250
00:17:15,600 --> 00:17:17,200
I'm going to pay for this one for sure, right?

251
00:17:17,600 --> 00:17:18,880
In real life, that's what would happen.

252
00:17:19,520 --> 00:17:28,400
So there were these decisions where we understood there was going to be friction, to be blunt, right, with users, but that we were comfortable with having that conversation.

253
00:17:29,360 --> 00:17:32,080
When we kind of wargamed it out, people are going to say that.

254
00:17:32,560 --> 00:17:36,000
And I said, not in lack of empathy, but so what?

255
00:17:36,480 --> 00:17:47,680
If you're a user who's looking to download this app and you're getting four one star reviews and it's four people who are upset that they had a thing for free for almost a decade and now they have to pay, you know, 60 bucks.

256
00:17:48,400 --> 00:17:55,360
And I understand the star rating, like that's important, but we have to make this shift here because I think for every four that complain, ten are going to subscribe.

257
00:17:55,600 --> 00:17:57,040
And that's basically what happened.

258
00:17:57,360 --> 00:17:59,040
So there's a little bit of blunt force there.

259
00:17:59,360 --> 00:18:07,120
And that was a, I guess the data in that case, we're more mature now, we don't have to make bigger bets like that or a little bit bigger leaps that we just hope.

260
00:18:07,520 --> 00:18:10,480
But there the data was we're gaming out those conversations, right?

261
00:18:11,120 --> 00:18:15,280
And trying to draw a line between reasonable and unreasonable, which is completely subjective.

262
00:18:15,840 --> 00:18:20,640
And which conversations are we comfortable with and which ones are we not, and that was one that we were comfortable with.

263
00:18:20,800 --> 00:18:22,400
So you really like burned the bridges.

264
00:18:22,640 --> 00:18:36,560
Because this I had this exactly same debate and I landed on the other side of it for my app that, if I recall correctly, part of this decision was that as a business, you needed to make money, like you needed to move the dial.

265
00:18:37,440 --> 00:18:37,840
financially.

266
00:18:38,400 --> 00:18:52,240
And had you only made it free trial for new users and grandfathered in this really passionate long term cohort of users, you knew you were going to leave too much money on the table that you needed to propel the business forward.

267
00:18:52,320 --> 00:19:02,960
So it was also like a business strategy decision that we need the money and we're willing to take it on the chin to get the cash flow we need to keep moving the business forward.

268
00:19:03,360 --> 00:19:03,440
Yeah.

269
00:19:03,840 --> 00:19:06,080
You know, a lot of people like call us greedy.

270
00:19:06,240 --> 00:19:10,400
And if I showed you the financial history of this company, that's the kind of hilarious statement.

271
00:19:10,800 --> 00:19:12,400
So yeah, we just made that.

272
00:19:12,400 --> 00:19:14,000
We just did that leap, David.

273
00:19:14,400 --> 00:19:19,920
It's like almost not a lot of thinking required to explain the background behind it.

274
00:19:20,240 --> 00:19:23,680
We needed to do it for the financial stability, viability of the company.

275
00:19:24,160 --> 00:19:26,880
We just can't run if it's a nonprofit, it's a different story.

276
00:19:27,200 --> 00:19:40,720
We're still in a process of moving users over to new pieces of software and sometimes my team asks and I jump in to a conversation and I say, look, like we're running a business and these people are working hard and they deserve to be paid.

277
00:19:41,200 --> 00:19:41,280
Yeah.

278
00:19:41,600 --> 00:19:42,480
So we offer a service.

279
00:19:42,800 --> 00:19:47,680
If the service isn't for you, then find a different service or don't choose not to consume this service.

280
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:48,720
That's really that.

281
00:19:49,280 --> 00:19:49,360
simple.

282
00:19:50,160 --> 00:19:53,520
Maybe a different way of saying it is you can't please everyone.

283
00:19:54,720 --> 00:20:00,800
Much harder when those people are already in the tent and some may perceive it as you kicking them out because you're changing the rules of the game.

284
00:20:01,520 --> 00:20:03,920
No one liked doing this to be super clear.

285
00:20:04,320 --> 00:20:05,680
I was like, ah, we got them.

286
00:20:05,840 --> 00:20:07,680
You know, like, let's squeeze these guys, right?

287
00:20:07,920 --> 00:20:08,640
Like, not at all.

288
00:20:09,040 --> 00:20:12,560
But to run a viable business, you're going to have to make some decisions like that.

289
00:20:13,040 --> 00:20:26,400
And I would just say, for as hard as it is in your case, right, and in mine with your weather app, it's no different, I think, maybe it's a little bit of encouragement if someone's looking down the barrel of that to having set the right stuff in the first place, right?

290
00:20:26,960 --> 00:20:37,680
If you say you can only come into the theater if you buy a movie ticket and then there's people in the theater that didn't pay, they got in a back door or whatever, you going and seeking them out and saying, hey, you have to pay to be here, that is a conflict.

291
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:38,320
I get it.

292
00:20:38,880 --> 00:20:44,720
You're much less likely to do it in that scenario than the person at the front door, but fundamentally you should, right?

293
00:20:44,880 --> 00:20:48,000
Like you owe it to your shareholders, you owe it to your business and the people that work there.

294
00:20:48,160 --> 00:20:51,840
You can give them better raises, we can invest more in the product for the users that are paying.

295
00:20:52,320 --> 00:20:52,880
You can go down that.

296
00:20:53,280 --> 00:20:56,560
It's just kind of a matter of fairness, and that's not a word that's really in my vocabulary.

297
00:20:56,720 --> 00:20:59,360
But if you're going to use our thing, you've got to pay for it.

298
00:20:59,600 --> 00:21:02,800
And if we change the permissions, if you will, it also helps.

299
00:21:03,200 --> 00:21:04,480
You know, my wife's a doctor.

300
00:21:05,360 --> 00:21:06,480
I work for a golf company.

301
00:21:06,880 --> 00:21:08,640
It's like it's a golf app, right?

302
00:21:08,960 --> 00:21:12,800
This is not throwing you out of the hospital and you need life saving procedure or something, right?

303
00:21:13,120 --> 00:21:13,200
Wow.

304
00:21:14,560 --> 00:21:22,560
But then I guess it speaks to the core product market fit that you had these users using those free features for so long, for nine, like you said, nine years.

305
00:21:22,880 --> 00:21:24,480
Somebody's using something for nine years.

306
00:21:24,720 --> 00:21:26,880
It's because it's delivering a ton of value.

307
00:21:27,520 --> 00:21:33,680
So you already talked about you took it in the chin review wise, but then like, how did it go?

308
00:21:34,240 --> 00:21:35,680
Was it like an incredible success?

309
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:36,000
Yeah.

310
00:21:36,400 --> 00:21:40,080
Yeah, I mean, it grew 80, 90 percent in twelve months or something in terms of revenue.

311
00:21:40,320 --> 00:21:42,640
So yeah, there was just a lot of you can just do the math, right?

312
00:21:42,720 --> 00:21:45,040
And you go, wow, there's a lot of people are using the heck out of this.

313
00:21:45,440 --> 00:21:47,280
That transition was about future use.

314
00:21:47,520 --> 00:21:50,960
It wasn't about holding people's data hostage and so on and so forth.

315
00:21:51,520 --> 00:22:02,720
And one lesson I do think that we've learned is that when we try to communicate a nuanced message with three points, the first point is heard and the other two are not.

316
00:22:03,440 --> 00:22:04,480
That's because people are busy.

317
00:22:04,640 --> 00:22:05,760
It's not like people are stupid.

318
00:22:05,840 --> 00:22:06,800
That's not what I'm saying.

319
00:22:07,120 --> 00:22:15,360
But really, repeatedly, we've spent a lot of time when we do these disruptive things because this has been a turnaround, whatever the right word is, right?

320
00:22:15,600 --> 00:22:17,520
But a rethink, whatever the right phrase is.

321
00:22:17,920 --> 00:22:24,880
There's been a lot of disruption and we're still disrupting in other ways and other lines of business, et cetera, to try to get everything in the same strategy on the right path.

322
00:22:25,280 --> 00:22:34,000
And it's just funny, maybe funny is the wrong word, but how repeatedly there will be a nuanced thing that we're trying to say to folks and really take away one point and they move on.

323
00:22:34,160 --> 00:22:34,960
There's a recent example.

324
00:22:35,200 --> 00:22:36,480
I'll spare you the details.

325
00:22:36,880 --> 00:22:48,720
So I guess my point is, if you're looking at one of these decisions and you believe that the aggressive decision is the right one, just know that the five weeks you're going to spend massaging, well, no, there should be a fifth email.

326
00:22:49,760 --> 00:22:50,560
that communicates this.

327
00:22:51,120 --> 00:22:52,240
It is a little bit binary.

328
00:22:52,640 --> 00:22:54,720
If you're going to move someone's cheese, you're moving it.

329
00:22:54,880 --> 00:22:56,240
That's the way they're going to perceive it.

330
00:22:56,560 --> 00:23:04,080
The fact that you did it to the 95th percentile versus the 55th percentile is likely lost because they're upset that the cheese has moved.

331
00:23:04,320 --> 00:23:07,440
I don't know if that points to don't do that extra work.

332
00:23:07,840 --> 00:23:12,960
I'm just saying it's unlikely that it will be recognized if you move, change the game on someone.

333
00:23:13,440 --> 00:23:15,680
So just buyer beware, fair warning.

334
00:23:16,080 --> 00:23:16,960
You won't be praised.

335
00:23:17,120 --> 00:23:19,920
Well, I know you had to do this, but you did it in the best possible way.

336
00:23:20,320 --> 00:23:22,560
You're not going to get the first part, so you won't get the second part.

337
00:23:23,200 --> 00:23:25,840
So how did you communicate it because that's a big deal.

338
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:40,640
Again, why I wanted to have you on and why I wanted to talk to this is because I think there are a lot of apps out there making decent money, not growing, struggling that should not necessarily go from freemium to really burn the boats and bridges there.

339
00:23:40,960 --> 00:23:50,000
It would be hard for me in good faith recommend a lot of apps go quite so dramatic, but hey, if you're struggling as a business, you need to make bigger bets.

340
00:23:50,320 --> 00:23:51,200
And this was a big bet.

341
00:23:51,520 --> 00:23:57,360
So then if people are going to make a big bet and make a big change, especially to existing users, how did you communicate it?

342
00:23:57,600 --> 00:24:00,480
And it sounds like you've you've already shared one lesson, but any other lessons?

343
00:24:01,120 --> 00:24:03,520
in that communication that people could take away from?

344
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:07,440
Well, I think the eight thousand twenty rule applies, right?

345
00:24:08,160 --> 00:24:14,080
And I think fewer better customers are better in general.

346
00:24:14,720 --> 00:24:15,840
It's a general rule.

347
00:24:16,160 --> 00:24:21,840
If we could pile in a million users, but they were all on the knife edge at every renewal, right?

348
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:23,280
And we knew they didn't really get value.

349
00:24:23,600 --> 00:24:24,720
We call them zombies, right?

350
00:24:24,880 --> 00:24:27,840
Like we got a lot of zombie users out there that maybe they forgot.

351
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:30,560
I mean, that's a dirty secret of the subscription business, right?

352
00:24:30,880 --> 00:24:33,680
Is that everyone has some percentage of users?

353
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:40,640
This is why consumers revolt against subscriptionscriptions, they forget that they even have the subscription, right?

354
00:24:40,880 --> 00:24:45,280
And no one wants to talk about that if you're in our shoes, but that's a part of the business model, right?

355
00:24:45,920 --> 00:24:56,640
And one thing that gave us confidence, I think, was this, which is that if you have fewer better customers, you're going to spend less time trying to manage churn, you spend less time just like worrying, right?

356
00:24:56,960 --> 00:24:59,520
About how tenuous your grasp is on these folks.

357
00:25:00,160 --> 00:25:04,560
What if the people that are really getting value out of it have to pay for the value, right?

358
00:25:04,720 --> 00:25:06,800
Again, that's a theme of simplification, right?

359
00:25:07,440 --> 00:25:11,360
So that was what I think gave us a lot of solace.

360
00:25:11,600 --> 00:25:12,880
And so it comes down to segmentation.

361
00:25:13,680 --> 00:25:15,200
and trying to understand your customers.

362
00:25:15,520 --> 00:25:22,640
If you believe you have a customer, lots of customers that actually love the product, you should charge a fair price for it.

363
00:25:23,360 --> 00:25:35,920
If you're worried, so, so worried that this applies to price increases too, so, so worried that a ten percent price increase, maybe not today's, but the last couple of years inflationary environment, is going to run off all your customers.

364
00:25:36,400 --> 00:25:38,640
I would just encourage you to think through why.

365
00:25:39,600 --> 00:25:45,920
Is it because you don't have conviction that your product is really solving one of these people's problems, right, or your users' problems?

366
00:25:46,400 --> 00:25:48,800
If that's the case, the problem isn't the price increase.

367
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:53,760
The problem is, right, understanding those customers and delivering the value, right?

368
00:25:54,080 --> 00:25:58,560
I think this comes down to trying to treat people like they are people and not users, right?

369
00:25:58,960 --> 00:26:05,920
Because we have all this data and thousands of users and so on and so forth, everything becomes a spreadsheet, but we forget that fundamentally it's people.

370
00:26:06,320 --> 00:26:15,360
And so I have to spend, like I said, a lot of time in other people's shoes, but then also a lot of time pretending like these interactions are happening in real life, one on one.

371
00:26:16,560 --> 00:26:17,360
Does that make sense?

372
00:26:17,760 --> 00:26:18,720
Would you do that in real life?

373
00:26:19,120 --> 00:26:20,480
Look, everyone knows it's software.

374
00:26:20,640 --> 00:26:22,000
It's programmed to give you this answer.

375
00:26:22,320 --> 00:26:22,720
That's fine.

376
00:26:23,280 --> 00:26:29,520
But if it doesn't feel right or doesn't imitate the human behavior, the chances of success are lower, in my opinion, right?

377
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:36,240
So again, to the person who has that issue with the nine years free, whatever, let's play this out in real life.

378
00:26:36,400 --> 00:26:37,040
Are we okay with that?

379
00:26:37,200 --> 00:26:39,520
Are we okay with other people overhearing that?

380
00:26:40,800 --> 00:26:42,400
The answer is yes, right?

381
00:26:42,800 --> 00:26:44,960
Whatever percentage of people are not going to like it, right?

382
00:26:45,120 --> 00:26:47,760
And that will create that snowball effect that you're so worried about, sure.

383
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:50,080
But most people, we think that's reasonable.

384
00:26:50,240 --> 00:26:52,960
These guys are working, they're delivering a service and they're not getting paid for it.

385
00:26:53,040 --> 00:26:54,720
They should get paid for it, probably, right?

386
00:26:55,360 --> 00:27:02,240
Segmenting your customers, understanding where they're coming from and what they're doing, and then are we scratching the itch that they have right can give you a lot of confidence.

387
00:27:02,400 --> 00:27:06,240
All that being said, I don't feel like every day I wake up and go, I don't think we understand this well enough, right?

388
00:27:06,400 --> 00:27:08,640
So it's not like you reach a point, oh, we got it.

389
00:27:08,800 --> 00:27:09,680
We figured them all out.

390
00:27:09,840 --> 00:27:10,480
Mission accomplished.

391
00:27:10,720 --> 00:27:12,000
We've landed on the moon, whatever.

392
00:27:12,160 --> 00:27:16,960
No, if you're not paranoid about that all the time, but that's the root cause issue of everything, right?

393
00:27:17,280 --> 00:27:20,320
Are you delivering the value to these people that they feel they can't live without?

394
00:27:20,640 --> 00:27:23,680
And if you are, you have a lot of freedom and flexibility to do a lot of things.

395
00:27:23,920 --> 00:27:27,840
So our average user spends 21 minutes a month in the app or monthly active users.

396
00:27:27,920 --> 00:27:28,720
That's a ton, right?

397
00:27:28,960 --> 00:27:31,680
Which is not a license to abuse them, but it is okay.

398
00:27:32,160 --> 00:27:34,640
These people get it and they're going to be retained at a high level.

399
00:27:34,800 --> 00:27:35,920
It's something to build on, right?

400
00:27:36,400 --> 00:27:40,560
And those that are in and out real fast or not using at that level, we want to understand why.

401
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:47,840
But we also know that a lot of those people are like not savable, not the ideal customer because there are ideal customers over here.

402
00:27:48,320 --> 00:27:49,280
What's working for these people?

403
00:27:49,520 --> 00:27:55,760
Let's really drill in here and understand and apply it to more with the understanding that these people might only be 20 percent, right?

404
00:27:56,320 --> 00:27:57,760
Everyone is not your ideal customer.

405
00:27:58,240 --> 00:28:06,160
You teed up segmentation, which I do want to dig into, but just one more time, restating the question, what did you tell them though?

406
00:28:06,400 --> 00:28:12,720
This is the like practical takeaway for folks of like, if you're going to make a big change to the way you operate.

407
00:28:12,720 --> 00:28:14,800
to ask people to pay.

408
00:28:15,200 --> 00:28:20,000
Did you just say like, you know, this business will fail and you won't have an app.

409
00:28:20,800 --> 00:28:21,840
