809
00:57:25,520 --> 00:57:30,960
99% of the time, if you're built like you are, David, or me, like, can you focus on something for longer than two hours?

810
00:57:31,120 --> 00:57:31,520
No way.

811
00:57:31,920 --> 00:57:33,920
Longer than two minutes, maybe, right?

812
00:57:34,480 --> 00:57:38,480
Most organizations, you multiply that difficulty by many people really can't.

813
00:57:38,720 --> 00:57:45,360
So not to talk out of both sides of our mouths and not focus too much on conversion or whatever at the outset, but also really focus on it.

814
00:57:45,680 --> 00:57:48,320
If you're convinced of two things, I think you're in trouble.

815
00:57:48,640 --> 00:57:52,000
One, you have a commoditized product and there's a lot of competitors doing something similar.

816
00:57:52,160 --> 00:57:56,000
And two, you've rung the rock for every ounce of efficiency everywhere around you.

817
00:57:56,640 --> 00:57:59,200
I don't think most people are in that boat.

818
00:57:59,600 --> 00:58:02,000
It certainly makes marketing et cetera more challenging.

819
00:58:02,160 --> 00:58:06,880
And so you do need to at least come up with a genuine, this is why we're different, right?

820
00:58:07,280 --> 00:58:07,520
Statement.

821
00:58:07,760 --> 00:58:12,320
Even if it's on soft things, like we actually answer the phone for support or we've been in the business for a long time.

822
00:58:14,080 --> 00:58:15,600
You can find those differentiating factors.

823
00:58:15,680 --> 00:58:19,600
So even if the core may be very similar, but yeah, I would challenge that.

824
00:58:20,080 --> 00:58:24,560
The assumption that if you're commoditized or you think your thing is commoditized, that's like, that's the end of the road.

825
00:58:25,440 --> 00:58:28,240
Because I guarantee that there are ways you can execute better.

826
00:58:28,800 --> 00:58:30,320
There's a lot of room to run there.

827
00:58:30,480 --> 00:58:36,320
Again, if your goal is to be a trillion dollar market cap company, yeah, your thing has to be the latest Nvidia chip.

828
00:58:36,560 --> 00:58:37,280
Don't get me wrong.

829
00:58:37,440 --> 00:58:37,600
Right?

830
00:58:37,760 --> 00:58:38,640
And that just is what it is.

831
00:58:39,040 --> 00:58:44,160
But there's a lot of space between no growth and bonkers growth to move.

832
00:58:44,480 --> 00:58:47,360
And you might just have to be realistic that it's not going to be a thousand X every year.

833
00:58:47,600 --> 00:58:53,040
But if it's ten percent every year, twenty percent every year, reliably because you keep getting better at what you're doing, that might be a happy spot.

834
00:58:53,200 --> 00:58:55,920
But yeah, obviously, highest and best is finding a new thing and making it better.

835
00:58:56,080 --> 00:58:59,600
But that's the shiny object syndrome that many of us are prone to fall into.

836
00:59:00,240 --> 00:59:00,320
Yeah.

837
00:59:00,800 --> 00:59:12,720
But I think this whole podcast has been a great lesson on how to actually find that differentiation as you think about users, have those conversations in your head with actual users.

838
00:59:12,880 --> 00:59:13,440
I've never done that.

839
00:59:13,600 --> 00:59:16,640
That's I'm going to, that's one key takeaway I'm going to do.

840
00:59:16,720 --> 00:59:18,560
It's like I've done user testing.

841
00:59:18,800 --> 00:59:32,160
I have had those user conversations, but I don't regularly look at a feature, look at a dialogue box that I'm creating, look at marketing copy, and then picture someone standing in front of me and make it into a conversation.

842
00:59:32,640 --> 00:59:38,960
I think it's a really great way to operate as you're building a consumer product is to like put that consumer right in front of you and think about it.

843
00:59:39,280 --> 00:59:47,760
So I think this whole conversation has been a great way to focus you on not chasing that shiny object syndrome, but actually finding the things that users care about.

844
00:59:48,080 --> 00:59:55,440
So thanks for sharing all your lessons and getting there and growing and burning the ships and everything you've done along the way.

845
00:59:55,600 --> 00:59:57,600
Because I think this is going to be super helpful to folks.

846
00:59:57,680 --> 00:59:58,320
So yeah, thanks.

847
00:59:58,400 --> 00:59:59,200
Thanks so much for sharing.

848
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:00,720
Thank you for inviting me, David.

849
01:00:00,800 --> 01:00:06,160
I love to reflect, and it's a work in progress, and there's no destination that has been arrived at, just to be super clear.

850
01:00:06,560 --> 01:00:13,840
Anything you wanted to share as we wrap up, do you have any job openings that might be a good fit for folks or any way the audience can interact with you or help you?

851
01:00:14,160 --> 01:00:20,800
If anything, any of this was stimulating, you want to have a conversation, experience shared, whatever, Alex at V one Sports, pretty easy.

852
01:00:21,120 --> 01:00:22,000
Yeah, just reach out.

853
01:00:22,720 --> 01:00:23,280
Have a chat.

854
01:00:23,680 --> 01:00:25,760
Happy to learn more from, you know, more people in this space.

855
01:00:26,000 --> 01:00:27,840
Well, thank you so much for joining me, Alex.

856
01:00:28,240 --> 01:00:29,040
This was so much fun.

857
01:00:29,200 --> 01:00:29,680
Thanks, David.

858
01:00:29,680 --> 01:00:33,840
Thanks so much for listening.

859
01:00:34,800 --> 01:00:38,160
If you have a minute, please leave a review in your favorite podcast player.

